\code{model} argument is not a tracked name in the article: \code{solve(Some_Model, ev)}
is not recognised unless the article also passes \code{"Some_Model"} to
\code{readModelDb()} or \code{modellib()}.

\strong{Lagged-dose bioavailability.} When the model has an \code{alag()} statement
and an \code{f()} whose right-hand side depends on a covariate (directly, through
intermediate assignments, or through an \verb{if()} condition), every simulation
in the article -- each \code{rxSolve()} call and each rxode2 \code{solve(model, ...)}
call -- must state \verb{addlKeepsCov =} or \code{nonmem = TRUE}. A lagged dose is
applied when it arrives, and which record's covariates its \code{f()} reads then
depends on the setting and on the dose type (see \emph{Which setting}), so the
result changes when a covariate changes between occasions. The contract is
to resolve or report every form the rule can read, and to list here what it
cannot.

\emph{Which setting.} Measured on rxode2 5.1.8.9000 (commits e4cb67f6 and
c865aa8), with a different value on each candidate record: the one before
the dose, the dose's own, and one other record at the dose's time or inside
the lag window. For a bolus dose, \code{addlKeepsCov = FALSE} (the default) reads
the record in force when the dose arrives, so a record at the dose's time
(written before or after the dose) or inside the lag window wins over the
dose's own record, while \code{addlKeepsCov = TRUE} and \code{nonmem = TRUE} read the
dose record's own covariates, which is NONMEM's pairing. For an infusion
(\code{rate = -2} with \code{dur()}, \code{rate = -1} with \code{rate()}, or a fixed rate),
\code{TRUE} and \code{nonmem = TRUE} never read the dose's own record. \code{rate = -1} and
\code{-2} read the last record before the dose, or a record at the dose's time
other than a dose into the same compartment (an observation, or a dose into
another compartment), which then supplies \code{f()}. A fixed rate (\code{rate} above
0) reads a record that cannot be pinned to the dose or its neighbours: with
records at 0, 10 and 20 and a dose at 24, \code{f()} came from the record at 0, not
the one at 20; with the first record at 5, 3 or 1 it came from the dose's own
record; a record at the arrival time (24.5) or just after it (24.7, lag 0.5)
supplied it, and one at 26 or 40 did not; how far after the arrival a record
reaches depends on the dose's amount. \code{FALSE} reads the dose's own record for
every infusion type unless a record other than a dose into the same
compartment shares the dose's time, which then supplies \code{f()} whatever the row
order (a bolus ignores a dose into another compartment at its time, an
infusion reads it); a record inside the lag window does not override it. A
fixed duration (a \code{dur} column, no \code{rate}) reads like a bolus. A modelled
\code{rate()} or \code{dur()} comes from a record at the dose's time or inside the lag
window (not at the arrival time itself) under every setting, except under
\code{nonmem = TRUE} when that record's row is written before the dose: an
observation supplies it, so can a dose into another compartment, and a dose
into the same compartment does not. Without a lag every setting reads the
dose's own record. What is right for every dose type is an event table in
which each record carries the covariates of the dose that opened its
interval, with \code{addlKeepsCov = FALSE}; the rule accepts \code{TRUE} and
\code{nonmem = TRUE} too, but they match NONMEM only for bolus doses. The rule
checks that the setting is stated, not the event table.

\emph{What counts as a simulation.} \code{rxSolve()} in any spelling (\verb{rxode2::},
\code{rxSolve.default}, native or magrittr pipes, arguments over several lines).
\code{solve()} and the methods rxode2 registers (\code{solve.rxUi()} and the others)
count when an argument traces to a model through the article's assignments: a
name assigned from \code{readModelDb()}, \code{modellib()}, \code{rxode2()} or \code{rxode()}; a \code{model()} /
\code{ini()} / \code{zeroRe()} pipe of one; the \verb{$ui} of one; an element (\code{$}, \code{[[}) of
a list of models, including one built by \code{lapply()}, \code{purrr::map()} or
\code{setNames()} over their names; the loop variable of a \code{for} loop over such a
list, the parameter of a function mapped over it (\code{lapply()}, the \code{purrr}
\code{map} family, \code{walk()}, ...), or the variable of a \code{foreach()} loop; a
\code{with(list(m = mod), ...)} binding; the model's library name. A \code{solve()}
inside an article-local function on one of its parameters counts when the
function is called with a model there, and is reported at the definition.
\code{base::solve()} on a matrix is left alone, and so is a \code{solve()} the article
defines itself at top level (its own \code{rxSolve()} call is judged instead; a
definition inside a function or \code{local()} shadows nothing). A name assigned
something that is not a model stops being one; an \code{if}/\verb{else}, \code{switch()},
\code{tryCatch()} or \code{local()} that yields a model in every branch is a model.
\code{mod$simulate()} and \code{mod$solve()} are not rxode2 methods and are ignored.

\emph{What counts as stating the setting.} \verb{addlKeepsCov =} with any value but
\code{NULL} (a variable counts: the author addressed \code{addlKeepsCov} itself), or a
literal \code{nonmem = TRUE} (or \code{T}), among the call's own arguments or inside an
\code{rxControl()} (or \code{control = list()}) argument. \code{nonmem} counts only as a
literal \code{TRUE}, because it sets \code{addlKeepsCov} only when it is \code{TRUE} and a
variable may hold \code{FALSE}; \code{nonmem = FALSE}, \code{NULL} and \code{nonmem = flag} do not
count. Arguments of other nested calls do not count either:
\code{foo(addlKeepsCov = TRUE)} inside an \code{rxSolve()} call is \code{foo}'s argument.
\code{control = ctl}, with \code{ctl} defined elsewhere, is not followed, and the call
is reported as not verifiable rather than as unstated. For an indirect call
(\code{do.call(rxSolve, list(mod, ev, addlKeepsCov = TRUE))},
\code{lapply(mods, solve, ev, nonmem = TRUE)}) the setting is looked for among its
arguments and in a \code{list()} or \code{c()} among them.

\emph{Reported as not verifiable} (unless the call itself states the setting): an
\code{rxSolve()} or model \code{solve()} whose \verb{control =} is a variable or any call
other than \code{rxControl()} / \code{list()}, since the setting inside it cannot be
read; a call to \code{rxSolve} fetched from an object
(\code{asNamespace("rxode2")$rxSolve(...)}, \code{ns[["rxSolve"]](...)});
\code{nlmixr2(..., est = "rxSolve")}, or an \code{est} that is not a literal;
\code{simulate()}; \code{rxSolve} or \code{solve} handed to another function as a value
(\code{do.call()}, \code{lapply()}, \code{Map()}, \code{match.fun()}, ...) -- \code{solve} only when
it travels with a model, a list of models, an event table or an
rxSolve-only argument, since \code{lapply(matrices, solve)} is matrix algebra;
a \code{solve()} with event-table-like arguments (an \code{et()} call, a data frame of
dosing records, a function that builds one, \verb{atol =}, \verb{params =}, ...) but no
traceable model; and a chunk that mentions a solver but does not parse (a
bare \verb{solve(} counts only when the article also uses \code{readModelDb()}, \code{modellib()} or the
model's name).

\emph{Not resolved and not reported.} Only these forms are silent whatever the
arguments: a call built from text (\code{eval(parse(text = ...))}) and one fetched
through a function the rule does not follow
(\code{getFromNamespace("rxSolve", "rxode2")(...)},
\code{getS3method("solve", "rxUi")(...)}). Others are reported only when their
arguments are event-table-like, and are
otherwise read as matrix algebra: a model returned by a user function; a
model handed to a local function through \code{lapply()} or \code{do.call()} rather than
called directly; \code{do.call(solve, args)} with \code{args} a bare name. A \code{solve()}
whose model cannot be traced and whose arguments are not event-table-like is
read as matrix algebra. Names are tracked across the whole article rather than
per function. A record built inline with \verb{amt =} or \verb{evid =} counts as an
event table even in \code{solve(rbind(amt = ..., time = ...))}.

\emph{Over-approximations} (reported although the rule may not apply, and cleared
by stating the setting). \code{alag()} and \code{f()} are not tied to the same
compartment. Assignments are not ordered: every right-hand side a variable is
ever given counts, including one read before it is assigned. An article that
uses several models takes the union of their covariates, so a simulation of a
model without the hazard is reported when another model in the article has
it. Covariates that never change between occasions (weight, sex) are
reported like any other; stating \code{addlKeepsCov = FALSE} is the intended way
to clear them. A wrapper that forwards \code{...} into \code{rxSolve()} is reported at
its definition even when every caller states the setting, since the setting
cannot be seen flowing through.

\strong{Lagged-dose pairing.} For a model with an \code{alag()} compartment whose
\code{f()}, \code{rate()} or \code{dur()} reads a covariate, the rule reads the event table
each \code{rxSolve()} is given and reports (\code{lagged-dose-pairing}) every lagged
dose for which a record the setting may read the covariates from carries
different values than the dose's own record: the records are those in the
table in \emph{Which setting} (a record at the dose's time, one inside the lag
window, the record before the dose, ...), and a difference in any of them is
reported, because which one wins depends on the dose type and the row order.
The finding says how many lagged doses are affected and names one: its time
and subject, the covariate, both values and the record. The setting is the
one the call states as a literal (\code{addlKeepsCov}, \code{nonmem}, in an inline
\code{rxControl()} or \code{control = list()}), \code{FALSE} when it states none, and all
three when it cannot be read (a variable, \code{tieOrder}, an unnamed extra
argument that may be a control object, or an \code{options()} call that sets an
rxode2 option anywhere in the article). The lag time of each dose is the
model's \code{alag()} evaluated at the initial estimates and the dose record's
covariates. When that cannot be computed, or the lag reads a random effect
(which differs between subjects), or the call supplies parameters that may
change it (\code{params} or \code{theta} that is not a literal naming other things,
\code{thetaMat}, \code{iCov}, an unnamed extra argument), the lag is not known and the
window is every record up to the dose's next dose: a lag shorter than the
dosing interval cannot reach further, so a table whose records up to the next
dose all carry the dose's covariates is clean, and a difference is reported
with a note that the lag was not known. A lag longer than the dosing interval
is not seen then. A fixed
rate under \code{TRUE} or \code{nonmem = TRUE} may read any record of the subject, so
any difference is reported. The table that makes every \code{FALSE} setting clean
is the one in \emph{Which setting}: each record carries the covariates of the dose
that opened its interval.

\emph{How the event table is obtained.} The code that builds it is run: the
statements before the call that define what the events argument reads (found
by name, so a statement that defines nothing needed is not run), the code
before the call in the blocks, loops and function it is inside, the
parameters of a helper function bound from its top-level calls (its defaults,
\code{...}, and a helper handed to \code{lapply()} by name included), the first value
of a loop variable and the first element of what \code{lapply()}, \code{purrr::map()},
\code{Map()} or \code{purrr::pmap()} is applied to. It runs in a sandbox: the only
functions it can find are the article's own and a fixed list from base, stats,
utils, tibble, tidyr, purrr, dplyr and rxode2's event-table builders
(\code{et()}, \code{add.dosing()}, ...), so a call to anything else (file access,
\code{system()}, \code{eval()}, \code{readModelDb()}, \code{rxSolve()}, a model) is refused before
anything runs, whatever spelling reaches it, and is reported as not
verifiable. Functions that evaluate a string argument as a glue template or as
the name of a function outside the list (\code{tidyr::pivot_wider(names_glue =)},
\code{pivot_longer()}, \code{unnest_longer()}, \code{dplyr::rename_with()}, \code{stats::ave()})
are left out of it for that reason; the list was audited by calling each
function with hostile payloads in every parameter. A run is limited to 20 seconds and 3 million rows, an article to
20 simulations, and the session's random-number stream is put back
afterwards. This guards an article's own code against its accidents; it is
not a boundary against hostile code.

\emph{Reported as not verifiable} (\code{lagged-dose-pairing-unverifiable}, one finding
per reason naming the first simulation and counting the rest): an event table
built with a call the sandbox does not run, which includes any dependence on
the model object (\code{readModelDb()}, \code{rxode2()}, \code{zeroRe()}, \code{ui$omega}); a
table with \code{addl} or steady-state (\code{ss}) doses; a table that is not a data
frame or event table, has no numeric \code{time} column or is empty; a build that
fails or runs over the limits; a simulation that is not an \code{rxSolve()} or
\code{solve(model, ...)} call whose arguments can be read (\code{do.call()},
\code{nlmixr2()}, \code{simulate()}); a helper the article never calls at the top level,
or an anonymous function whose parameter is not one the rule binds; a model
that cannot be read or analysed; the model changed by \code{model()} in the
article; and, with several models that have a lagged dose, a simulation whose
model cannot be told. A simulation of another
library model (read from a different literal name) is not about the lagged
dose and is skipped.

\emph{What it does not check.} Doses expanded by \code{addl}, because the pairing of an
expanded dose was not measured. Next to another dose the measurements are
partial (a bolus's \code{f()} and a dose into another compartment): a dose at the
dose's time or inside the lag window is a candidate for every quantity, a dose
at its time makes the next record after it a candidate for \code{rate()} and \code{dur()}, and
so is an earlier dose still in its lag when the dose is recorded (a lagged dose
into another compartment supplies \code{rate()} and \code{dur()} only if it is recorded
no earlier than the dose and arrives no later, or is recorded earlier and
arrives later). A loop or an apply-family function is run for its first element
only. The compartment of a numeric \code{cmt}
is its position among the model's states. A covariate the table does not
carry as a column (an \code{iCov}, a constant) cannot differ between records and
is not compared.

\emph{Over-approximations.} Every record a dose may read counts, so a table whose
last-written record in the window carries the dose's own covariates is still
reported when an earlier one in the window does not. A typical-value solve
(\code{zeroRe()}) of a model whose covariate acts through an eta is reported
although its effect is nil. Covariates that never change between occasions
are never a difference, so they are not reported.
}
